    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d713">

                <pb facs="0608" n="596" sameAs="frus:frus1942v04/pg596"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">890D.01/594: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Beirut</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Engert</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Beirut</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-05-06T11:00:00+02:00">May 6, 1942—11
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 2:18 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>158. Since my 155, May 1, I have seen President of Syria and his Foreign Minister
                    in Damascus. Also Maronite Patriarch in Lebanon. Their response was most
                    encouraging and all agreed when I told them we must forget all personal
                    ambitions and petty political rivalries in order to defeat common foe as quickly
                    as possible.</p>
                <p>Incidentally Spears has not yet heard from London about memo in your April
                        28.<anchor n="19" xml:id="d713-f1"/> I have briefly mentioned to him some of
                    my reasons for preferring not to give it our official support immediately.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Engert</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d713-f1" n="19" xml:id="d713-f1-content">
                <p>See telegram No. 57, April 28, 10 p.m., to the Consul General at Beirut, <ref
                        target="#pg593">p. 593</ref>.</p>
            </note>
